    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. One specific issue is that the current clause is quite narrow and doesn't explicitly mention \"government-mandated closures\" or \"civil unrest,\" though the disruption was quite chaotic. If the event falls outside the specific list provided in the agreement, is there a legal doctrine or a way to argue that these unforeseen circumstances should be treated as force majeure anyway? I'm looking for how a court might handle that gap in the text.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a force majeure clause lacks explicit mention of certain events, such as government-mandated closures or civil unrest, the interpretation of 'force majeure' might extend beyond the express terms under the legal doctrine of \"deviation from normalcy.\" Courts can apply a broad interpretation when the consequences of the unforeseeable event are similar in substance to the listed events, which essentially focus on the fundamental purpose of force majeure provisions—the relief from responsibility for events beyond a party’s control that prevent contractual fulfillment.\n\n\nIn arguing that the gap in the text should encompass the disruptive event, one might rely on the concept of \"commercial impracticability.\" This doctrine acknowledges that performance under the contract has become excessively burdensome due to circumstances that were unforeseen and not allocated to either party, making it unfair to impose obligations under such conditions.\n\n\nTo strengthen the argument, you might present evidence of the unpredictability and the severity of the event, as well as its similarity to naturally occurring events listed under the clause. Furthermore, show how the spirit of the agreement aimed to address such unforeseen and disruptive situations. If you can establish that these additional events would likely have been covered under the clause had the drafter done a full analysis, the court may interpret the clause in favor of extending force",
      "source": "target",
