    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. You mentioned the \"spirit of the agreement\" and how courts look at the fundamental purpose of the clause. If the disruption feels almost random or unpredictable in a way that's hard to document—sort of like a series of small, chaotic interruptions that collectively make it impossible to operate—how does a lawyer bridge that gap between \"unpredictable chaos\" and a formal legal argument for force majeure? Is there a standard way to categorize \"unforeseeable chaos\" in a legal brief?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
